        <h2>Dry Shampoo Starch Type Selection and Particle Sizing</h2>
        <p>Selecting the appropriate dry shampoo starch type is critical for oil absorption capacity and sensory properties. The ideal starch must have high porosity, a low moisture content, and a narrow particle size distribution. If the particles are too large, they scatter light and leave a noticeable white residue on dark hair. If they are too small, they can become inhaled hazards and pack tightly, clogging the valve cup. For a complete analysis of ingredient selection in cosmetic spray products, read our guide on <a href="https://www.globalaerosols.com/cosmetic-aerosol-hair-products" target="_top">cosmetic aerosol hair products</a>.</p>
        <p>Formulators often choose modified starches to optimize hydrophobic oil affinity. Aluminum starch octenylsuccinate, for example, is modified to introduce hydrophobic chains, improving sebum absorption. Untreated natural starches like corn starch tend to clump under humid conditions due to hydrogen bonding between glucose chains. Hydrophobic modification reduces this clumping, ensuring that the powder particles remain separate and flow freely through the dip tube during actuation.</p>

