      <!-- Section 3 -->
      <section id="performance" class="section">
        <h2>Rice Starch vs Corn Starch Aerosol Performance</h2>
        <p>When comparing a rice starch vs corn starch aerosol, rice starch is preferred for high-end formulations due to its small particle size. Rice starch granules range from 2 to 8 micrometers, while corn starch granules average 12 to 20 micrometers. This difference in size changes how light scatters off the hair surface. The smaller rice starch particles distribute evenly, absorbing oil while remaining nearly invisible to the naked eye. In contrast, larger corn starch particles reflect more light, creating a white cast that is difficult to brush out of dark hair. To understand the physical properties of surfactants and additives in suspensions, consult <a href="https://www.globalaerosols.com/aerosol-chemistry-101" target="_top">aerosol chemistry 101</a>.</p>
        <p>The small size of rice starch also increases its surface-area-to-volume ratio, giving it a higher sebum absorption rate per gram. However, these fine particles are prone to agglomeration inside the can due to electrostatic forces. To prevent this, formulators include anti-caking agents like colloidal silica or magnesium stearate. These agents coat the starch particles, creating physical barriers that keep them separated and free-flowing.</p>

        <div class="box" style="border-left: 4px solid var(--primary); background: #f0f9ff; padding: 20px; margin: 24px 0; border-radius: 0 var(--radius-md) var(--radius-md) 0;">
