          Rice starch particles (2–8 μm) offer the best balance of high oil absorption and minimal white residue. To prevent these fine particles from clumping, formulators add light anti-caking agents that coat the starch grains.
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 4 -->
      <section id="suspension" class="section">
        <h2>Suspension Chemistry and Non-Aqueous Solvent Carriers</h2>
        <p>The stability of a dry shampoo aerosol formulation depends on suspension chemistry and the selection of the non-aqueous carrier solvent. Because starch is insoluble in liquefied propellants, the particles tend to settle over time. According to Stokes' Law, the sedimentation rate is determined by the particle radius, the density difference between the solid and liquid phases, and the viscosity of the liquid carrier. Because liquefied propellants have low densities (around 0.5 to 0.6 g/cm³) and low viscosities, starch particles (density ~1.5 g/cm³) settle quickly. To study how manufacturing facilities handle raw material properties and quality testing, refer to <a href="https://www.globalaerosols.com/cosmetic-aerosol-manufacturing" target="_top">cosmetic aerosol manufacturing</a>.</p>
        <p>To slow this settling, formulators add volatile solvents like denatured alcohol (Ethanol) or light esters. These solvents increase the viscosity of the liquid phase and act as carriers for the starch. Rheological modifiers, such as disteardimonium hectorite, are also added to build a weak gel network inside the can. This gel keeps the starch particles suspended during storage, but breaks down under the shear forces of shaking, allowing the product to dispense cleanly. The solvent must also evaporate quickly upon spraying; if it evaporates too slowly, it will wet the hair, defeating the purpose of a dry shampoo.</p>
      </section>

