      <!-- Section 5 -->
      <section id="propellants" class="section">
        <h2>Dry Shampoo Propellant Interaction and Atomization Physics</h2>
        <p>The selection of the dry shampoo propellant dictates the pressure profiles and spray characteristics of the final product. Liquefied hydrocarbons, such as butane, isobutane, and propane mixtures, are the standard choice for dry shampoos. These gases have high solubility in alcohol carriers, which reduces the viscosity of the liquid phase. During spray discharge, the propellant evaporates rapidly, breaking the liquid stream into a fine dry shampoo powder spray. Propellant choices also carry implications for VOC regulations and environmental compliance.</p>
        <p>In regions with strict limits on Volatile Organic Compounds (VOCs), manufacturers are adopting alternative propellants. Dimethyl ether (DME) and hydrofluoroolefins like HFO-1234ze are being used to reduce carbon emissions while maintaining sufficient pressure for proper atomization. However, DME has higher polar solvency than hydrocarbons, which can cause swelling in standard valve gaskets. To prevent leaks, manufacturers use compatible elastomer gaskets like butyl or chloroprene rubber. The valve actuator must also feature a mechanical breakup nozzle to ensure the powder is dispersed evenly, preventing clumping.</p>

        <div class="box" style="border-left: 4px solid var(--amber); background: #fffbeb; padding: 20px; margin: 24px 0; border-radius: 0 var(--radius-md) var(--radius-md) 0;">
          <strong style="color: var(--amber); display: block; margin-bottom: 6px; font-family: 'Outfit', sans-serif;">Rule of Thumb</strong>
          When formulating with alternative propellants like DME, run long-term valve gasket swelling tests. DME can cause standard EPDM seals to swell, leading to valve clogging and product leaks.
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 6 -->
      <section id="testing" class="section">
        <h2>Laboratory Characterization and Oil Absorbing Aerosol Testing</h2>
        <p>To bring a dry shampoo formulation to market, laboratory characterization and safety testing are essential. The dynamic behavior of the suspension is monitored using optical analyzers to measure sedimentation rates under accelerated conditions. Spray pattern analysis and particle size distribution are also measured to ensure the spray remains uniform over the life of the can. These testing protocols must align with global safety standards, including the <a href="https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX%3A31975L0324" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">EU Aerosol Dispensers Directive</a>, which governs pressure tolerances, filling volumes, and labeling for commercial aerosols.</p>
        <p>Efficacy is evaluated by measuring the sebum absorption capacity of the starch on synthetic hair swatches. Using infrared spectroscopy, researchers track the reduction of lipids on the hair fibers after treatment. Additionally, flammability testing, including ignition distance and enclosed space ignition tests, is mandatory to meet hazard classification requirements. These evaluations ensure that the dry shampoo is safe for consumer use and performs consistently under different storage conditions.</p>
      </section>

