      <section id="faq" class="box" style="background: #ffffff; border: 1px solid var(--border); border-radius: var(--radius-lg); padding: 30px; margin: 40px 0;">
        <h2 style="margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 24px; font-family: 'Outfit', sans-serif; color: var(--text-primary);">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
        
        <div style="display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 16px;">
          <div style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border); padding-bottom: 16px;">
            <h4 style="font-size: 1.05rem; font-weight: 700; margin-bottom: 6px; color: var(--text-primary);">Which starch is preferred for a dry shampoo aerosol formulation to minimize visible residue?</h4>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; line-height: 1.6; color: var(--text-muted); margin-bottom: 0;">Oryza Sativa (rice) starch is highly preferred in dry shampoo aerosol formulations due to its exceptionally small and uniform particle size, typically ranging from 2 to 8 micrometers. This small particle diameter allows the starch to distribute evenly over the hair shaft, absorbing excess sebum while scattering less light, which dramatically reduces the unsightly white residue common with larger-grained starches.</p>
          </div>

          <div style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border); padding-bottom: 16px;">
            <h4 style="font-size: 1.05rem; font-weight: 700; margin-bottom: 6px; color: var(--text-primary);">How does dynamic viscosity affect the suspension stability of dry shampoo concentrates?</h4>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; line-height: 1.6; color: var(--text-muted); margin-bottom: 0;">The stability of the non-aqueous powder suspension inside the aerosol can is governed by Stokes' Law. Since the starch particles do not dissolve, dynamic viscosity of the liquid carrier must be carefully optimized, often using rheological modifiers like disteardimonium hectorite. This prevents rapid sedimentation, hard-packing, and valve clogging during storage.</p>
          </div>

          <div style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border); padding-bottom: 16px;">
            <h4 style="font-size: 1.05rem; font-weight: 700; margin-bottom: 6px; color: var(--text-primary);">What are the common propellant options for dry shampoo aerosols?</h4>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; line-height: 1.6; color: var(--text-muted); margin-bottom: 0;">Liquefied hydrocarbon propellants such as propane, butane, and isobutane blends are standard due to their low density and high vapor pressure, which aid in flash atomization. Increasingly, eco-friendly low-GWP options like dimethyl ether (DME) or HFO-1234ze are being adopted to comply with regional VOC standards while maintaining sufficient pressure for proper powder dispersion.</p>
          </div>

          <div style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border); padding-bottom: 16px;">
            <h4 style="font-size: 1.05rem; font-weight: 700; margin-bottom: 6px; color: var(--text-primary);">Why are specific ingredient percentages omitted in dry shampoo formulation discussions?</h4>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; line-height: 1.6; color: var(--text-muted); margin-bottom: 0;">Exact ingredient percentages and phase ratios are kept confidential as proprietary trade secrets to protect intellectual property. Technical resources focus instead on ingredient functions, particle size characterizations, solvent polarity parameters, and suspension mechanisms to explain how dry shampoos perform without revealing commercial manufacturing recipes.</p>
          </div>

          <div style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border); padding-bottom: 16px;">
            <h4 style="font-size: 1.05rem; font-weight: 700; margin-bottom: 6px; color: var(--text-primary);">How does sebum absorption capacity vary among different starch types?</h4>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; line-height: 1.6; color: var(--text-muted); margin-bottom: 0;">Sebum absorption capacity is determined by the starch's porous surface structure and oil absorption index. Modified starches like aluminum starch octenylsuccinate exhibit high hydrophobic affinity, drawing oil rapidly. Rice starch has a high surface-area-to-volume ratio due to its tiny size, yielding excellent absorption weight-for-weight compared to larger corn or potato starches.</p>
          </div>

          <div style="padding-bottom: 8px;">
            <h4 style="font-size: 1.05rem; font-weight: 700; margin-bottom: 6px; color: var(--text-primary);">What quality control tests are critical for dry shampoo aerosols?</h4>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; line-height: 1.6; color: var(--text-muted); margin-bottom: 0;">Critical tests include pressure-testing according to the EU Aerosol Dispensers Directive (75/324/EEC) and spray pattern characterization to ensure uniform particle dispersion. The sedimentation rate is monitored under accelerated aging, and shake-and-spray checks are performed to verify that the suspension redisperses cleanly without packing the dip tube or clogging the valve.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

